# Phase 1 — exposure gates

Phase 1 made the engine's existing polarization physics reachable from the
bindings: `pol_set`/`pol_get`, `vecdif_set`, `coat_set`/`coat_get` and
`rayfield_get` in `macos_api_mod`, surfaced as `macos.polarization`,
`macos.vector_diffraction`, `macos.coating` and `macos.ray_field` in both
mmacos and pymacos. The gates here are state, round-trip and invariance
checks; the physics gates start in Phase 2.

## 1.1 Geometry invariance under `ifPol`

**Claim.** Turning polarization on does not perturb the ray geometry.

This is a *prerequisite*, not a nicety. The Jones pupil is assembled by
pairing rays across two traces taken with different input polarization
states. That pairing is only meaningful if the two traces agree ray-for-ray,
so this gate is what makes the whole Phase-2 construction safe.

**Measurement.** `Rx_Cass_FarField.in` traced to the focal plane with
polarization off and then on, comparing the OPD map and the per-ray status
array.

| Quantity | Measured | Expected |
|---|---|---|
| max &#124;ΔOPD&#124; | @@G11_OPD_DIFF@@ waves | 0 |
| OPD bitwise equal | @@G11_BITWISE@@ | 1 (true) |
| max ray-status difference | @@G11_STATUS_DIFF@@ | 0 |

**Pinned by** `tPolarization` (state and round-trip) plus this driver for the
OPD and status comparison.

## 1.2 Coating round-trip

**Claim.** `coat_get ∘ coat_set` is the identity in physical thickness units.

The waves-at-parse-wavelength normalization described in *Conventions* is an
internal storage detail: it is applied on set and inverted on get, so a user
of the API never sees it. This gate is what makes that claim checkable.

| Quantity | Measured | Expected |
|---|---|---|
| max relative round-trip error, @@G12_NLAYER@@-layer stack | @@G12_COAT_ROUNDTRIP@@ | 0 |

**Pinned by** `tPolarization/test_coat_roundtrip_identity`.

## 1.3 Polarization-off is a no-op

**Claim.** None of the polarization work perturbs a polarization-off
prescription.

**Measurement.** The GMI regression suite, whose prescriptions are all
polarization-off: @@X_GMI@@ *(external, captured 2026-07-26 — see
`external.json`)*.

This is the guard that lets polarization land on a shared engine branch
without a compatibility argument: the results are bit-identical, not merely
close.
